<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Maqpon dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">1190 CE – 1840 CE (approx.) · capital Skardu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The long-ruling dynasty of Baltistan, whose rajas governed Skardu high in the Karakoram for six centuries until the Dogra conquest.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Maqpon tradition holds that the dynasty was founded around 1190 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-shah">Ibrahim Shah</a>, a foreign prince who married into the local ruling house of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skardu">Skardu</a>. The early centuries are known only from later Balti chronicles and genealogies, which present the line as continuous rulers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> valley around Skardu.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-sher-khan-anchan">Ali Sher Khan Anchan</a>, in the late 16th and early 17th centuries, was the most famous ruler: he campaigned into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-ladakh">Ladakh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilgit">Gilgit</a> and is credited with building the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skardu">Skardu</a> fort and with polo's spread in the region. The population converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, mainly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/noorbakhshia">Noorbakhshi</a> and Shia, from the 14th century. The rajas traded and intermarried with Ladakh and acknowledged Mughal suzerainty for a time.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1840 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dogras">Dogra</a> general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zorawar-singh">Zorawar Singh</a>, serving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulab-singh">Gulab Singh of Jammu</a>, conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltistan">Baltistan</a> and deposed the last Maqpon raja, Ahmad Shah. The region became part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/jammu-and-kashmir-princely-state">Jammu and Kashmir</a> and since 1948 has been administered by <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/islamic-republic-of-pakistan">Pakistan</a> as part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilgit-baltistan">Gilgit-Baltistan</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/maqpon-dynasty.jpg" alt="Maqpon dynasty" width="320" height="226"><figcaption>Image: S SHER XAMAN photography · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shangrila_resort_skardu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1190 CE – 1840 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Skardu</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ali Sher Khan Anchan, Ahmad Shah</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Shia Islam, Noorbakhshia</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Balti</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/pakistan">Pakistan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Baltistan, Maqpons of Skardu</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1515&amp;focus=maqpon-dynasty" aria-label="Maqpon dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6593 901 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Maqpon dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6913 1157L7018 1385M6913 1157L6918 1285"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7018 1385h0M6918 1285h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6913 1157h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6913 1157h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1515&amp;focus=maqpon-dynasty">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

